# The radio's network, read-only, and the pair of screens it only means anything against.
#
# Filmed rather than described because the section is an argument about *where* a fact belongs
# rather than about any one row: the Radio tab's details say what the interface is doing
# (connected, at this address, syslog on), and this says what it was told to do. The scene looks
# at this one, which is the comparison somebody makes when a radio is configured
# for a network it never joined. The demo radio is on the network it was told to join, so the
# two agree here; what the scene shows is that both are now askable.
#
# The static rows are here because the demo radio is on a static address; under DHCP the
# heading and the four below it are not drawn at all.
scene demo
scale 4
delay 180
config

tab settings
hold 400
key down 9
hold 600
key a
hold 1100
key down 6
hold 900
key b
hold 600
